{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbDfqS2777LUvSyi2Gx9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate for this exploratory slot as `worst_stretch_switch_deficit`, and it passes the admission self-check (prints `OK`). The check only runs a 100-draw smoke fit, so I haven't verified that it converges or that its fit is any good. The novelty check against the other models also hasn't run.\n\n**Hypothesis:** people judge a sequence by its single worst-looking stretch. For every contiguous stretch of flips, they compare how many times it switches between heads and tails with how many their own ideal switching rate predicts. Too few switches is a streak; too many is rigid alternation. The sequence is only as random as its worst stretch. The gap is counted in switches rather than as a rate, so a long stretch that is a little off can outweigh a short one that is badly off. This fits the brief's \"one striking stretch decides\" lens. Each person has their own ideal switching rate and guesses on some trials at their own rate.\n\n**Why I chose it:** I fitted quick logistic regressions to all the responses pooled together. This one feature, with a fixed ideal of 0.7 switches per flip, scored about as well as the three cues the current best models use combined: negative log-likelihood 2994.8 vs 2995.6 (lower is better). Those cues are alternation distance from an ideal, heads/tails imbalance and longest run. Two other forms did worse:\n- the overall switching rate alone: 3040.5\n- the same worst-stretch idea measured as a rate rather than a count: 3173 or worse\n\nSo it is one mechanism with three population-level parameters (ideal, sensitivity, lapse), not a blend of cues.\n\n**Things to watch:**\n- **PSIS-LOO looks unreliable:** on the smoke fit, 7% of trials had a high Pareto k (the tolerance is 1%), so that ELPD of -2549.8 is not trustworthy yet. If the full fit has the same problem, the model can't be exported as the winner, though it can still be admitted.\n- **Speed:** the self-check took about 13 minutes, probably because other candidates were running at the same time. One gradient evaluation takes 4.8 ms, which works out to roughly 5 minutes per chain for a full fit, inside the 30-minute limit. That is an estimate; I haven't run a full fit.\n- **Critique:** it doesn't directly address the palindrome finding in `critiques.md`. It does give a mechanism by which an opening streak can count for as much as a closing one, which bears on the initial-run finding.\n\nThe three files are in `.../experiment2/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_0/`: `hypothesis.md`, `model_name.txt` and `candidate.py`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1163,"cache_read_input_tokens":78297,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1163,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6aeaaca0-f0ed-4202-a7f9-3394aef7020e","uuid":"b7ad5c5b-a805-4b18-a44a-919d02b30653","timestamp":"2026-10-01T08:41:57.526Z","request_id":"req_011CfbDfpyV479Bwsbnt9MWG"}
